  "id": "COMPOUND:992 - PROTEIN:585",
  "verbalization": "Ketoprofen is implicated in the pathway leading to Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase through multiple indirect routes. The evidence suggests that Ketoprofen acts as a target for several enzymes, including Solute carrier family 22 member 6 and Neutral alpha-glucosidase C, which in turn target Caprylic acid. Caprylic acid is known to interact with Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, indicating a potential metabolic pathway involving fatty acids. Additionally, Ketoprofen is linked to D-aspartate oxidase, which targets 2'-Hydroxymatteucinol, which also interacts with Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. These findings suggest that Ketoprofen may influence the activity of Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase through a series of enzymatic interactions, possibly via fatty acid metabolism. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Ketoprofen in modulating glycolytic processes, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
